Month = {APR},
Abstract = {Recently, the outbreak of the SARS-CoV-2 virus and the COVID-19 pandemic
   significantly affected the health situation of the entire society and
   necessitated reorganization of health care including oncology. The
   objective of this study was to examine the perception of medical
   services by cancer patients during the pandemic and to identify the key
   elements influencing the level of satisfaction with oncological care. Of
   note, 394 patients diagnosed with cancer treated in inpatient oncology
   wards participated in the study (Poland). The diagnostic survey method
   was used. A survey questionnaire developed by the authors was used and
   validated the EORTC IN-PATSAT32 questionnaire. The calculations were
   made in Statistica 10.0 (Statsoft; 2011, Dell Inc., Round Rock, TX,
   USA). The average general level of satisfaction with oncological care in
   the study group was 80.77 out of a total score of 100, representing the
   highest level of satisfaction. Levels of satisfaction varied according
   to time since diagnosis (longer time-greater satisfaction) and were
   lower where treatment was delayed or perceived as disorganised. Nearly
   half of the respondents felt the threat of the SARS-CoV-2 infection,
   despite the fact that most of them believed that the hospital was well
   prepared to diagnose and treat cancer patients during the COVID-19
   pandemic. Convincing patients about the proper preparation of health
   care for diagnostics and therapy is an important element influencing
   patient satisfaction with oncological care.},
