   Fontenot, Mary Catherine},
Title = {College student sleep quality and mental and physical health are
   associated with food insecurity in a multi-campus study},
Journal = {PUBLIC HEALTH NUTRITION},
Year = {2021},
Volume = {24},
Number = {13},
Pages = {4305-4312},
Month = {SEP},
Abstract = {Objective: To assess the relationship between food insecurity, sleep
   quality, and days with mental and physical health issues among college
   students. Design: An online survey was administered. Food insecurity was
   assessed using the ten-item Adult Food Security Survey Module. Sleep was
   measured using the nineteen-item Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI).
   Mental health and physical health were measured using three items from
   the Healthy Days Core Module. Multivariate logistic regression was
   conducted to assess the relationship between food insecurity, sleep
   quality, and days with poor mental and physical health. Setting:
   Twenty-two higher education institutions. Participants: College students
   (n 17 686) enrolled at one of twenty-two participating universities.
   Results: Compared with food-secure students, those classified as food
   insecure (43 center dot 4 \%) had higher PSQI scores indicating poorer
   sleep quality (P < 0 center dot 0001) and reported more days with poor
   mental (P < 0 center dot 0001) and physical (P < 0 center dot 0001)
   health as well as days when mental and physical health prevented them
   from completing daily activities (P < 0 center dot 0001). Food-insecure
   students had higher adjusted odds of having poor sleep quality (adjusted
   OR (AOR): 1 center dot 13; 95 \% CI 1 center dot 12, 1 center dot 14),
   days with poor physical health (AOR: 1 center dot 01; 95 \% CI 1 center
   dot 01, 1 center dot 02), days with poor mental health (AOR: 1 center
   dot 03; 95 \% CI 1 center dot 02, 1 center dot 03) and days when poor
   mental or physical health prevented them from completing daily
   activities (AOR: 1 center dot 03; 95 \% CI 1 center dot 02, 1 center dot
   04). Conclusions: College students report high food insecurity which is
   associated with poor mental and physical health, and sleep quality.
   Multi-level policy changes and campus wellness programmes are needed to
   prevent food insecurity and improve student health-related outcomes.},
